    "body": "Copper cathode production in electrowinning plant. ( Stock image by Jorge. )\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 2, 2026\nChile’s copper commission has cut its production forecasts for the world’s largest producer and raised its 2027 price outlook by 17% as mines struggle to recover lost output.\nCochilco raised its average copper price forecast for next year to $5.95 (C$8.32) per lb. from $5.10 in releases issued Friday. It now expects Chilean output to fall 8.4% this year, compared with the 2.6% decline projected in August.\n“The [global] surpluses we project are small and can change with operational delays or higher-than-expected consumption,” Executive Vice-President Claudia Rodríguez said in a price announcement Friday.\nThe projected surplus of refined metal leaves little room for further supply disruptions. Demand just 1% above next year’s forecast would erase the surplus and create a deficit of about 65,000 tonnes if supply stayed unchanged, according to calculations based on Cochilco’s figures.\nBMO Capital Markets analysts Helen Amos and George Heppel said the downgrade could leave Chile with its weakest annual copper production in more than two decades.\nChile’s decline\nCochilco raised its average price forecast for this year to $6.18 per lb. from $5.95. It still expects prices to ease 3.7% next year as supply recovers, but that decline depends on mines meeting their production plans and metal reaching consumers.\nChile is expected to produce 4.94 million tonnes of the red metal this year and 5.08 million tonnes next year, down from the agency’s August forecasts of 5.27 million and 5.55 million tonnes, respectively.\nThe country produced 3.63 million tonnes in this year’s first nine months, down 8.5% from a year earlier. BHP’s (ASX, LSE, NYSE: BHP) Escondida and Spence operations, together with state-owned Codelco, accounted for 77% of the net national decline .\nCochilco points to lower ore grades, maintenance and interruptions affecting both the amount of material processed and the copper recovered. Reaching even the reduced annual forecast requires monthly production of about 434,000 tonnes in the fourth quarter, 7.6% above the average through September.\nCochilco forecasts global mine production will rebound 3.4% next year to 24.08 million tonnes after slipping 0.6% this year. Chile and the Democratic Republic of Congo would supply about 37% of that increase, making their recovery central to Cochilco’s market outlook. Chile would still produce 5.7% less copper than in 2025.\nUneven stocks\nCochilco forecasts a refined-copper surplus of 144,000 tonnes this year, equivalent to 0.5% of consumption, widening to 215,000 tonnes next year, or 0.8%. Higher refining output, supported partly by intermediate stocks and recycled material, can coexist with falling mine production, it pointed out.\nThe location of available metal adds to the strain. In its separate weekly market report, also released Friday, Cochilco recorded 1.05 million tonnes across the three principal exchanges. The U.S. exchange COMEX held almost 70% of the total, while Shanghai Futures Exchange stocks fell 18% during the week to 44,612 tonnes.\nChina’s Yangshan import premium reached $205 per tonne, while cash copper on the London Metals Exchange traded $210 per tonne above the three-month contract on Thursday. Those premiums reflect the higher cost of obtaining metal for immediate delivery.\nCochilco cautioned that fewer arrivals, shipping delays and contractual supply restrictions helped draw down Chinese stocks. The decline therefore doesn’t establish a broad acceleration in consumption.\nDemand offers less reassurance. The BMO analysts warned that AI investment is one of the few engines of industrial growth while construction and autos remain sluggish. A material slowdown in data-centre spending would leave few alternatives to cushion the impact on demand, they said.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: South America\nCOMPANY: BHP Cochilco Codelco\nRepublish this article",
